        with "Description: data value too large", which is how it was found (2026-08-03, during
        the winner-anchor change that had to add one fieldPermissions entry here). The content is
        operationally important, so it was moved rather than trimmed; a comment is repo-only and
        is never sent to the Metadata API, so it can be as long as it needs to be.
        🔴 Do not paste this back into <description>.

        VERIFY AFTER EVERY DEPLOY of this permission set:
            SELECT SobjectType, COUNT(Id) FROM FieldPermissions
            WHERE Parent.Name = 'Broker_Protection_Access' GROUP BY SobjectType
        must return Lead 32, Task 6, Contact 1, Inbound_Email_Staging__c 24,
        Competing_Broker_Submission__c 10, Property_Registry__c 3 (plus platform-managed Event
        mirrors of the Task/Activity rows).
        ⚠ Lead moved 28 -> 32 on 2026-08-14 with the Acquisition Observations Phase 1 intake
        fields: Listing_Status__c, Extraction_Score_Pct__c, Fields_Captured_Count__c and
        Fields_Missing_Count__c. A count of 28 after that change means the four new fields' FLS
        did not land. The pipeline still WRITES them correctly either way — the Lead insert is
        system-mode, so a missing grant does not block the write, it makes the values INVISIBLE
        to every human on this permission set. That is the harder failure to spot: the extraction
        score exists to tell an analyst which leads need chasing, and a blank score reads as
        "nothing to chase" rather than as "you cannot see this".
        ⚠ Note an asymmetry that is deliberate and should not be "corrected": Parse_Confidence__c
        is NOT granted on this set, while the three new score fields ARE. They are different
        things (see the fields' own descriptions) and the score fields were named for this set
        explicitly by the design. Do not add Parse_Confidence__c here to make them match, and do
        not remove the score fields to make them match either.
        ⚠ Competing_Broker_Submission__c moved 9 -> 10 on 2026-08-03 with Winning_Opportunity__c,
        the post-conversion winner anchor. A count of 9 after this change means the new field's
        FLS did not land, and the inbound pipeline will read a null anchor on every
        converted-winner property.
        ⚠ Inbound_Email_Staging__c moved 19 -> 22 on 2026-08-05 with Attachment_Count__c /
        Attachment_Bytes__c / Dropped_Attachment_Notes__c, the inbound attachment-capture feature.
        A count of 19 after this change means the three new fields' FLS did not land — the
        pipeline still writes the retained-file count/bytes/drop-reasons correctly (per the
        ordinary Metadata-API custom-field rule, deployed fields arrive with no FLS for anybody),
        they are just invisible to every human on this permission set, including the FLS-gated
        §2 B size-risk reporting the fields exist to enable.
        ⚠ Inbound_Email_Staging__c moved 22 -> 23 on 2026-08-06 with Attachment_Status__c, the
        v2 attachment-persistence redesign's sweeper work-queue field (design-requirements-
        attachment-persistence-v2.md §10.2 A1/A3). A count of 22 after this change means the new
        field's FLS did not land — the field is still stamped correctly by the pipeline, it is
        just invisible on this permission set. NOTE: Content_Publication_Budget__c (the new
        Hierarchy Custom Setting backing the L5/L4 quota guards, same design §6.6/§10.2 A2) is
        DELIBERATELY NOT declared anywhere in this file — Custom Settings need no FLS grant for
        Apex access, so a speculative fieldPermissions/objectPermissions block for it here would
        be dead weight, not a missing grant.
        ⚠ Inbound_Email_Staging__c moved 23 -> 24 on 2026-08-08 with Routing_Attempt_Count__c, the
        routing-resilience retry counter (design-requirements-routing-resilience.md §10 A2/A3).
        A count of 23 after this change means the new field's FLS did not land — the field is
        still written correctly by RoutingFailureFinalizer (system-mode DML), it is just invisible
        on this permission set. Status__c also gained a fourth restricted picklist value
        ('Failed') in the same change; that does NOT move this count — adding a value to an
        already-granted field's value set is not a new fieldPermissions row.

        🔴 Status__c.editable FLIPPED true -> false ON 2026-08-09 (code review C3) — DO NOT REVERT.
        'Failed' is a MACHINE-ONLY instruction: RoutingRetrySweepBatch replays any row it finds at
        Status__c = 'Failed' on the sole strength of isRetryableRoutingState's proof that an
        UNHANDLED_EXCEPTION rolls back its own transaction (§3.3 of the routing-resilience design).
        That proof holds only for rows the FINALIZER put there. A human with edit access on this
        picklist could hand-flip an 'Error' row (whose catch RAN, so its transaction may have
        already committed a Lead/claim/submission) to 'Failed', bypassing that guard entirely and
        handing the sweeper a row it must never touch — reproducing exactly the corruption §3.3
        catalogs (a second Lead, or the winning broker filed as a competing submission against
        their own Lead), recoverable only by manual registry surgery. Every Apex writer of
        Status__c (markRoutingFailed, markRoutingAbandoned, stamp — all in
        InboundEmailStagingService.cls) uses plain system-mode `update`, so this grant removes
        nothing the pipeline needs; it is a human-facing bypass only. The layout item for
        Status__c is Readonly for the same reason — see
        layouts/Inbound_Email_Staging__c-Inbound Email Staging Layout.layout-meta.xml. Field
        remains readable=true so a triager can still SEE the status.
        Routing_Attempt_Count__c is DELIBERATELY LEFT editable=true — resetting the counter alone
        cannot re-queue a terminal row (only a Status__c write can, and that is now locked), so a
        triager who has fixed the underlying deterministic cause of a Failed row's repeated deaths
        may legitimately want to buy it more attempts before RoutingRetrySweepBatch abandons it at
        the cap. If this reasoning is ever found wrong, lock it down the same way as Status__c.

        A redeploy has been observed to silently drop the Activity-field rows, which breaks the
        Email Service context user's (Minimum Access profile) ability to see the standard fields
        the inbound-email pipeline stamps: on Task, Description/ActivityDate/WhoId/WhatId; on
        Lead, Description/Phone/Title. Task.WhoId, Task.WhatId, Task.Description/ActivityDate and
        Lead.Description/Phone/Title are ALL now declared below (see the Task.WhoId comment
        further down for why that changed on 2026-08-06 — it used to be an org-side-only grant)
        and are expected to survive a redeploy; if the Lead count drops below 27, or the Task
        count drops below 6, after a deploy, that is a regression, full stop.

        🔴 ROOT CAUSE FOUND AND FIXED 2026-08-05 (unit-testing pass, attachment-capture review).
        The "Activity-field rows get silently dropped" symptom above was NOT a platform quirk —
        it was this file declaring `<field>Activity.Inbound_Message_Id__c</field>` /
        `<field>Activity.Thread_Key__c</field>`. `Activity` is not a valid SObject prefix for a
        <fieldPermissions> entry; on this org it deployed as an EVENT-only grant (measured:
        FieldPermissions grouped by SobjectType showed `Event 2` and `Task 3`, three short of the
        documented `Task 6`), so EVERY redeploy of this file silently wiped the Task-side FLS on
        both fields — exactly the "silently drop" behaviour the previous version of this comment
        described as an unexplained observation. Corrected to `Task.Inbound_Message_Id__c` /
        `Task.Thread_Key__c`, matching every other Task field declared below. Salesforce then
        auto-creates the "platform-managed Event mirror" the VERIFY query already expected, so
        Event access is not lost. If the Task count is ever short of 6 again after this fix, that
        is a REAL regression — the Activity-prefix mistake is what to check for first.

        🔴 SECOND INCIDENT, SAME ROOT CAUSE, FOUND AND FIXED 2026-08-06. The 2026-08-05
        Activity-prefix deploy above did fix Inbound_Message_Id__c/Thread_Key__c, but a Metadata
        API deploy of a PermissionSet REPLACES its entire field-permission set rather than merging
        with it — so that same deploy also wiped the org-side-only Task.WhoId row described below
        (created by hand 2026-08-01, never represented in this file). Every inbound email that
        routed to a Lead or Contact target then threw `System.DmlException | Operation failed due
        to fields being inaccessible on Sobject Task` out of
        `InboundEmailActivityService.logInboundEmail` (called from
        `ExtractAddressQueueable.finish`) — the Lead, the registry claim and the staging row still
        committed, but the Task never logged, so the pipeline lost both its activity-timeline
        entry and its `Inbound_Message_Id__c` redelivery-idempotency key for that email. Checked
        every other Task field the pipeline writes
        (Subject/Description/ActivityDate/Status/TaskSubtype/Inbound_Message_Id__c/Thread_Key__c/
        WhoId/WhatId) against `sobjects/Task/describe`: Subject, Status and TaskSubtype are NOT
        FLS-controlled (`permissionable: false`) so they need no grant and were never at risk;
        Description/ActivityDate/WhatId/Inbound_Message_Id__c/Thread_Key__c ARE FLS-controlled and
        were still present; Task.WhoId was the only FLS-controlled field missing. Fixed by
        declaring Task.WhoId in this file (see the block below) instead of leaving it org-side-only
        — an org-side-only grant on a file that gets redeployed for unrelated reasons is exactly
        the failure mode that produced both incidents.

        ObjectPermissions must include UserExternalCredential read (1 row) — without it, any
        callout through the OpenAI Named Credential throws before sending, regardless of the
        stored key or header configuration.
